-- 192 — ground the oracle corpus in independently asserted reality.
--
-- WHY. Every verdict this substrate produces about itself is generated by the machinery that
-- did the work, so a self-confirming verdict is indistinguishable from a true one. Measured
-- 2026-08-05: a truncated LLM completion returns resolved:true and earns ALPHA on the arm that
-- produced nothing; a staged-but-unlanded edit graded reached:true; an oracle and its generator
-- shared a parse and agreed by construction; and the LLM-free drafting floor had NEVER executed
-- (0 occurrences in 48h) while looking healthy the entire time.
--
-- goal_verification_labels records a CONCLUSION (verdict + confidence + labeler) but never the
-- ground truth behind it. Nothing stored says WHAT was checked, WHAT was expected, or WHEN the
-- claim was made — so a label can be written after the fact from the very observation it grades.
-- These fields make a label carry its own evidence.
--
-- The load-bearing field is asserted_at. A claim registered BEFORE the work cannot be retrofitted
-- to match the outcome; that single ordering constraint is what separates an assertion from a
-- rationalisation. `source` names an authority the substrate does not author — git (it pushes but
-- cannot forge the remote), systemd, the filesystem, a third-party HTTP status, or journald.
--
-- journald deserves special mention: a log line PROVES A BRANCH EXECUTED. That is the probe class
-- that caught the never-firing drafting floor, and it attacks the dominant failure mode here —
-- declared-but-not-running — which no amount of code reading detects.
--
-- EVERY FIELD IS option<> AND NO ASSERT IS ADDED, DELIBERATELY. Migration 101 declared
-- `ASSERT $value IN ['human','automated']` on labeler; goal-host emitted 'deterministic'; every
-- oracle-label write 500'd; the caller was fire-and-forget, so the corpus's most trustworthy tier
-- died silently on 2026-07-20 and stayed dead until migration 183. A schema that can reject a
-- write is a schema that can silently amputate a feed. Grounding is additive: an ungrounded label
-- remains valid and simply carries no evidence.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE asserted_at ON goal_verification_labels TYPE option<datetime>;
-- When the claim was registered. Meaningful ONLY when it precedes the execution it grades; the
-- writer enforces that ordering, because a constraint expressed only in prose is not enforced.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE source ON goal_verification_labels TYPE option<string>;
-- The external authority consulted: 'git' | 'filesystem' | 'process' | 'http' | 'journal' |
-- 'human'. Intentionally NOT asserted — an unrecognised source must be stored and auditable
-- rather than rejected at the door (see migration 101 above).

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE probe ON goal_verification_labels TYPE option<string>;
-- The exact, replayable command or query. Replayability is the point: a third party must be able
-- to re-run it and get the same answer without trusting this system.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE expected ON goal_verification_labels TYPE option<string>;
-- The value asserted BEFORE the work ran. Never written from the observation.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE observed ON goal_verification_labels TYPE option<string>;
-- What the probe actually returned.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE evidence ON goal_verification_labels TYPE option<string>;
-- Raw probe output, truncated by the writer. Kept so a disagreement can be adjudicated from the
-- bytes rather than from a summary of them.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE grounded ON goal_verification_labels TYPE option<bool>;
-- Derived by the writer, never supplied by the caller: true iff the label carries an external
-- source AND a probe AND an expected value AND asserted_at precedes the execution. This is the
-- field to aggregate on. "Fraction of labels that are grounded" starts at 0% and is the honest
-- measure of whether this system can know anything about itself.

-- Read paths filter on these; without indexes the corpus scan grows with total labels.
-- IF NOT EXISTS, not OVERWRITE: `DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE` is proven on this engine (migration
-- 183 applied cleanly), but no migration in this repo has ever executed `DEFINE INDEX OVERWRITE`,
-- and unproven DDL here does not fail loudly — migration 040 used SurrealDB 3.x FULLTEXT against
-- this 2.3.3 engine, never parsed, and took an analyzer plus three indexes down with it silently.
-- Use only syntax this repo has actually run.
DEFINE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS gvl_grounded_idx ON goal_verification_labels FIELDS grounded;
DEFINE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS gvl_source_idx   ON goal_verification_labels FIELDS source;
